498:%\subparagraph{Parameterized complexity and kernelization.} We refer to the monographs~\cite{CyganFKLMPPS15,DowneyF13} for the introduction to the area.  A \emph{parameterized problem} is a language $L\subseteq\Sigma^*\times\mathbb{N}$, where $\Sigma^*$ is the set of strings over a finite alphabet $\Sigma$, and its instance is a pair $(x,k)$, where $x\in\Sigma^*$ and $k\in\mathbb{N}$ is a \emph{parameter}. A parameterized problem $L$ is  \emph{fixed-parameter tractable} (\FPT) if there exists an algorithm solving $L$ in $f(k)\cdot|x|^{\Ocal(1)}$ time for a computable function $f$. It is said $L$ is \emph{slicewise polynomial} (\XP) if $L$ can be solved in $|x|^{g(k)}$ time for a computable function $g$. The classes \FPT and \XP are the classes of fixed-parameter tractable and slicewise polynomial parameterized problems, respectively. To show that the existence of an \FPT algorithm for a parameterized problem is unlikely, it is standard  to prove that the problem is \W{1}-hard. This is done by constructing a \emph{parameterized reduction}, that is, a reduction running in $f(k)\cdot|x|^{\Ocal(1)}$ time for a computable $f$, from a \W{1}-hard problem.   
500:%A \emph{kernelization} (or \emph{kernel}) for a parameterized problem $L$ is a polynomial-time algorithm that for any instance $(x,k)$, outputs an instance $(x',k')$ of $L$ such that (i)~$(x,k)\in L$ if and only if $(x',k')\in L$ and (ii)~$|x'|+k'\leq f(k)$ for a computable function $f$. A kernel is \emph{polynomial} if $f$ is polynomial. It is known that a decidable parameterized problem is in \FPT if and only if it admits a kernel, but it is unlikely that all fixed-parameter tractable parameterized problems admit polynomial kernels. It is standard to exclude the existence of a polynomial kernel up to the assumption that \coNP$\not\subseteq$~\NPp by providing a \emph{polynomial parameter transformation}, that is, a parametrized reduction where $f$ is polynomial, from the parameterized problem for which such a kernelization lower bound is already established.
