The paper confirms the key distinction: a witness is not an arbitrary replay trace but an \(H\)-witness partition (equivalently, a compaction to reflexive \(H\)); exact checking is cheap. It also immediately rules out a Track-A claim for paths/linear forests, and warns that fixed small \(k\) has an \(n^{2k}\)-style XP search. I’m now comparing the paper’s actual hardness reductions and algorithms to decide whether there is a defensible Track A distribution or a genuinely useful Track B compression gap.
