472:For algorithmic results, we use the following useful property of bags in witness structures corresponding to vertices of degree~$1$.
491:A graph whose vertex set is a clique is called \emph{complete}. We let $K_n$ denote the complete graph on $n$ vertices. 
492:Slightly abusing notations, may call a complete graph a clique.
493:A \emph{cluster graph} is a graph whose connected components are complete graphs.
494:A \emph{split graph} is a graph $G$ whose vertex set can be partitioned into a clique $K$ and an independent set $I$. Notice that such a partition is not always unique, and one of the sets may be empty. However, we always assume that $K\neq\emptyset$.
495:An \emph{interval graph} is a graph isomorphic to the intersection graph of intervals of the real line. 
